.TH HOTSWAPD 8 "August 2026" "hotswapd 0.2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
hotswapd \- monitor and manage USB hot-swap modules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hotswapd
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR registry ]
.RB [ \-G
.IR gpiochip ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR line ]
.RB [ \-P
.IR devpath-prefix ]
.RB [ \-\-no-gpio-release ]
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hotswapd
is a user-space daemon that monitors USB device attach and detach events,
maintains module state, applies configured removable-storage policy, and
publishes state through the system D-Bus service
.BR org.postmarketos.HotSwap .
.PP
The systemd service runs the daemon in the foreground with
.BR \-f .
Without that option, the process daemonizes itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Remain in the foreground and write logs to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-c " registry"
Read module definitions from
.I registry
instead of the default
.IR /etc/hotswapd/modules.json .
.TP
.BI \-G " gpiochip"
Use the specified GPIO character device, or
.B auto
to locate the Raspberry Pi controller. The default is
.BR auto .
.TP
.BI \-L " line"
Use the specified GPIO line offset. The default is GPIO26, physical header pin
37 on the CM5 IO Board.
.TP
.BI \-P " devpath-prefix"
Associate the release contact with devices below this USB DEVPATH prefix. This
is required when more than one module is attached.
.TP
.B \-\-no-gpio-release
Disable GPIO safe-release monitoring.
.TP
.B \-v
Enable verbose logging.
.TP
.B \-vv
Enable debug logging.
.TP
.B \-h
Print command-line help and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The JSON registry maps USB vendor and product identifiers to categories,
attach and detach actions, and storage sync policy. A storage
.B mount
action may specify
.B options
and a
.B mount_point
whose
.B {device}
token expands to the selected block-device basename.
.SH GPIO SAFE RELEASE
Install a removable jumper or normally-closed release contact between physical
header pin 37 (GPIO26) and pin 39 (ground). The line is requested as an input
with a pull-up, rising-edge detection, and 50 ms debounce. The installed
contact holds the input low; opening it lets the pull-up drive the input high
and triggers release. Set the CM5 IO Board GPIO-voltage selector to 3.3 V for
normal HAT-header use, and do not connect the input to 5 V.
.PP
When opened, the daemon flushes and normally unmounts storage for the selected
module before marking it ready. Listen for the
.B ModuleReadyForRemoval
D-Bus signal before removing it. A
.B ModuleReleaseFailed
signal means removal is not safe. The contact does not itself enforce a delay;
the enclosure should prevent or discourage removal until ready indication is
visible.
.SH CLIENT COMMANDS
The
.B hsctl
client can inspect attached devices and the module registry:
.PP
.EX
hsctl list
hsctl registry
.EE
.PP
To register the VID/PID and inferred category of a currently attached device,
pass the DEVPATH reported by
.BR "hsctl list" :
.PP
.EX
sudo hsctl register <devpath>
sudo hsctl register <devpath> --replace
.EE
.PP
The optional
.BR --name ,
.BR --category ,
and
.B --description
arguments override detected metadata. Registration is restricted to root
callers by both D-Bus policy and a daemon-side UID check. The daemon locks and
atomically updates the registry; a new definition normally applies on the
device's next attachment.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hotswapd/modules.json
Default module registry.
.TP
.I /etc/dbus-1/system.d/hotswapd.conf
System D-Bus policy.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hotswapd.service
systemd service unit.
.TP
.I /run/hotswapd.pid
PID file used while the daemon is running.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The daemon exits with status 0 after an orderly shutdown or after displaying
help. Initialization, configuration, and unrecoverable event-loop failures
produce a nonzero status.
.SH LOGGING
Foreground mode logs to standard error. Daemon mode logs through syslog;
under systemd, messages are available with
.BR journalctl (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR udev (7),
.BR dbus-daemon (1)
